1
00:00:00,180 --> 00:00:01,650
Mint can feel cold

2
00:00:01,660 --> 00:00:03,190
without coming out of a fridge.

3
00:00:03,640 --> 00:00:04,129
How?

4
00:00:04,610 --> 00:00:05,120
Menthol,

5
00:00:05,390 --> 00:00:06,920
a compound found in peppermint,

6
00:00:07,190 --> 00:00:08,000
can activate a

7
00:00:08,010 --> 00:00:11,200
cold-sensitive channel called TRPM8.

8
00:00:11,930 --> 00:00:13,600
Cooling can activate it too.

9
00:00:14,090 --> 00:00:15,940
That gives us two different inputs,

10
00:00:16,210 --> 00:00:17,360
a temperature change

11
00:00:17,550 --> 00:00:18,360
and a chemical.

12
00:00:18,730 --> 00:00:19,740
Both can stimulate

13
00:00:19,790 --> 00:00:21,680
part of the same sensory machinery.

14
00:00:22,130 --> 00:00:23,580
So a cooling sensation

15
00:00:23,550 --> 00:00:25,040
does not by itself

16
00:00:25,150 --> 00:00:26,260
tell you the temperature

17
00:00:26,250 --> 00:00:27,700
of what you touched or tasted.

18
00:00:28,430 --> 00:00:30,780
The mint is not a tiny ice cube.

19
00:00:31,250 --> 00:00:32,820
Its chemistry can take another

20
00:00:32,850 --> 00:00:34,540
route to a cold-sensitive channel.

21
00:00:35,070 --> 00:00:35,960
Different input,

22
00:00:36,370 --> 00:00:38,100
a familiar cold sensation
